## Goal
Build a desktop automation agent that uses Windows-MCP (pip package: windows-mcp,
GitHub: CursorTouch/Windows-MCP) to control the Microsoft Teams desktop app
(Office 365) from within VS Code Copilot agent mode. The agent must be able to:
  1. Launch or focus the Teams desktop app
  2. Navigate to a specific channel or chat
  3. Read messages visible on screen
  4. Type and send a message
  5. Take a screenshot and return it as confirmation

---

## Environment assumptions
- OS: Windows 10 or 11
- Python: 3.13+
- uv is installed (preferred) or pip fallback is acceptable
- VS Code 1.99+ with GitHub Copilot extension and agent mode enabled
- Microsoft Teams desktop app is already installed and the user is signed in
- No changes to firewall/antivirus are expected — all communication is local stdio

---

## What to build

### 1. MCP server config: .vscode/mcp.json
Create this file in the workspace root. Use the correct VS Code key ("servers",
NOT "mcpServers" — those are for Cursor/Claude Desktop). Use uvx to run
windows-mcp so no global pip install is required:

{
  "servers": {
    "windows-mcp": {
      "type": "stdio",
      "command": "uvx",
      "args": ["windows-mcp"]
    }
  }
}

If uv is not available on this machine, provide a pip fallback variant using:
"command": "python", "args": ["-m", "windows_mcp"]
after: pip install windows-mcp

Do NOT invent any other config keys. Only use what the windows-mcp README documents.

---

### 2. Agent instruction file: .github/copilot-instructions.md
This file tells Copilot how to behave when the windows-mcp tools are active.
Write it with these rules:

- Always call Screenshot first to get visual context before taking any action
- Use Snapshot (use_vision=True) when you need to identify interactive element IDs
- Use App tool to launch or switch to Teams — do not hardcode coordinates to open it
- Use WaitFor to confirm Teams has loaded before proceeding (wait for window title
  containing "Microsoft Teams")
- Use Click with element IDs from Snapshot output — not hardcoded pixel coordinates
- Use Type to enter text into the message box
- Use Shortcut("Enter") to send the message
- After sending, take a Screenshot to confirm
- Never guess coordinates. If you cannot identify an element from Snapshot, say so
  and ask the user for clarification.
- Teams may open in browser fallback mode (Edge/Chrome). If that happens, use
  Snapshot with use_dom=True to read the web DOM instead of the accessibility tree.

---

### 3. Example task script: scripts/teams_send_message.md
Write a plain-English step-by-step script (not code — this is a Copilot prompt
chain) that demonstrates:
  - Opening Teams via the App tool
  - Navigating to a named channel (parameterised as CHANNEL_NAME)
  - Reading the last 3 visible messages from the chat pane
  - Composing and sending a message (parameterised as MESSAGE_TEXT)
  - Taking a final screenshot saved to screenshots/sent_confirmation.png

Use only tools that windows-mcp actually exposes:
  Screenshot, Snapshot, Click, Type, Shortcut, App, WaitFor, Scroll, Shell
Do not invent tool names.

---

### 4. README.md
Write a setup section covering:
  a. Installing uv if not present (winget install --id=astral-sh.uv)
  b. Installing windows-mcp (uvx windows-mcp — no separate pip install needed
     with uvx, it auto-installs on first run)
  c. Opening .vscode/mcp.json in VS Code and clicking the Start button that
     appears at the top of the file
  d. Switching Copilot Chat to Agent mode (the dropdown in the chat panel)
  e. Verifying the server is running: Ctrl+Shift+P → MCP: List Servers
  f. Known limitations:
     - windows-mcp cannot interact with UAC dialogs or any window running as
       Administrator (Windows UIPI security boundary — not a bug)
     - Teams must already be signed in; this agent does not handle auth flows
     - Headless/SSH sessions will fail — a real GUI session is required
     - If Teams opens in browser mode instead of the desktop app, DOM mode
       (use_dom=True in Snapshot) is needed

